Description: static analysis of shell programs using symbolic execution
 SaSh analyzes POSIX shell programs ahead of time, using symbolic
 execution to predict their effects on the file system and to find bugs
 before the program is ever run.
 .
 For example, it reports word splitting or empty variables that could
 lead to deletion of critical paths such as /*, and commands that could
 silently overwrite files, e.g. two mv commands to the same destination.
 .
 SaSh is the artifact of the SOSP'26 paper "Ahead-of-time Analysis of
 Shell Program Effects". Its Python dependencies (libdash, shasta, z3,
 pash-annotations) are bundled in a private virtualenv under
 /opt/venvs/asash.
